# Example -- four relations that ordinary prose runs together.
#
# This file performs no causal inference and claims none. What it demonstrates is
# separation: four relations that English writes with the same word ("linked to",
# "associated with", "drives") are four objects here, and no two of them share a
# canonical identity.
#
#   implies(P, Q)      written `P => Q`, a logical connective between propositions
#   associated(X, Y)   the variables are not independent          -- SYMMETRIC
#   correlated(X, Y)   they co-vary linearly                      -- SYMMETRIC
#   causes(X, Y)       intervening on X changes Y                 -- DIRECTED
#
# The two properties worth stating precisely, both of them tested:
#
#   * `correlated(X, Y)` and `causes(X, Y)` have different content hashes. One
#     cannot be substituted for the other, by a model or by a rewrite.
#   * `causes(X, Y)` and `causes(Y, X)` have different content hashes, while
#     `correlated(X, Y)` and `correlated(Y, X)` have the SAME one. Direction is
#     part of a causal claim's identity and is not part of a correlational one.

module Causality

rv rainfall : RandomVariable[NonNegativeReal]
rv umbrella_sales : RandomVariable[Natural]
rv ice_cream_sales : RandomVariable[Natural]

# Two consequences of one common cause. They are correlated and neither causes
# the other -- the classic case that a single "related to" predicate cannot tell
# apart from the line below it.
require correlated(umbrella_sales, ice_cream_sales)
require associated(umbrella_sales, ice_cream_sales)

# A directed claim, and a strictly stronger one. Nothing here proves it; the
# language's job is that stating it is visibly a different act from stating the
# correlation above.
require causes(rainfall, umbrella_sales)

# Independence is the negation of association and is a claim about the laws, so
# it is an ordinary proposition rather than an event.
require independent(rainfall, ice_cream_sales)

# Implication stays what it has always been: a connective between propositions,
# with no probabilistic content at all. It is in this file to be contrasted, not
# to be grouped with the three above.
param p : Probability
param q : Probability
require p > 0.5 => q > 0.5
